WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.160
Vsauce!

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:07.749
Kevin here, and I’m auctioning off the ultra-rare
PlayStation 5: Jake Roper “Prepare to Cry”

00:00:07.749 --> 00:00:13.379
edition, which evokes the frustration and
heartbreak of Jake’s fruitless attempts

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:16.710
at securing his very own PS5.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:22.640
With this edition, you’re buying countless
hours of entertainment -- and a monument to

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.119
one man’s pain.

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:30.699
I’m auctioning it using a system of sealed
bids -- the bidders submit their price and

00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:32.219
we determine a winner.

00:00:32.219 --> 00:00:34.270
I’ve got 4 bids…

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:35.270
Ready?

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:39.240
Okay bid number one...

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.670
Hideo Kojima?

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:45.440
MY GREAT FRIEND HIDEO KOJIMA WHO I MET FOR
9 SECONDS IN 2013?

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.930
Even HE can’t get a PS5?

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:57.790
Alright well, $400, that’s pretty undervalued
-- he must be a very patient and frugal man.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:06.410
The PS5 retails for $499, so if this bid wins,
Kojima is getting a serious deal.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:10.070
Alright, bid #2: BALLOON KEVIN!?

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.980
You are not Real Kevin!

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:13.370
How many times?

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:14.750
I am Real Kevin.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.040
How did you even get in here?

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.470
Well, Whatever.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.620
And what is your bid? $3?

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:23.620
$3.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:24.620
Okay.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:25.620
Balloon Kevin is a scammer.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:26.990
What a stupid bid.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:31.080
But I guess maybe he thought lowballing was
worth a shot.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.080
Whatever.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.080
Ugh.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.080
Moving on...

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:40.630
Bid #3: Oh, this bid is actually from Jake
himself, still desperately trying to get a

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.260
PS5. $550?

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.710
That, that’s fair.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:51.550
It’s a 10% premium on top of the going price,
which makes sense for something in such high

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:52.550
demand.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.110
Smart bid -- good luck!

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:58.320
Hopefully we can dry those Roper tears.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.740
And now to reveal the fourth and final bid.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.540
Drumroll please...

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:09.180
$8,000!? WOW!

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.590
That is..

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:11.790
THAT is a serious bid.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:15.530
Michael must really want this thing.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:16.530
Well, SOLD!

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:17.530
For $8,000.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.810
THAT is absolutely what you would call a..

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:20.810
Pro.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:21.810
Gamer.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:22.810
Move.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:28.420
Dramatically outbidding everyone to secure
the win, and here’s something that you don’t

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:31.120
realize is so genius about it.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.210
This isn’t a normal auction.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:35.210
No.

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:36.940
This is a Vickrey auction!

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:40.840
Which means the winner doesn’t pay the highest
bid.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.120
They pay the second highest bidder’s bid.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.190
In this case, that’d be Jake’s $550.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.320
Sorry Jake.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:54.570
You’re really gonna wanna stay hydrated
because those tears will continue to flow

00:02:54.570 --> 00:03:03.260
as Michael is playing his brand new PS5 after
only paying YOUR $550 bid.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:06.710
So, that’s that!

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:13.540
And as always thanks for watching… w….wait…

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:19.430
A really aggressive bid worked out this time,
yeah.

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:26.220
But overbidding on a Vickrey auction is actually
a really, really bad idea -- and virtually

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:33.490
no one realizes that the most popular auction
house in human history works this way.

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:37.549
Here’s a question: WHAT ARE AUCTIONS?

00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:44.060
The discipline of auction theory within economics
is built on a handful of basic auction formats.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:50.610
You’re probably used to auctions that start
low and people bid back and forth until there’s

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:52.490
only one bidder left.

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:59.630
The highest bid wins -- that’s an English
auction, or an “open-bid ascending auction”

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:01.850
if you wanna get TECHNICAL.

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:05.540
You can also do auctions the other way, as
well.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:12.410
A Dutch auction starts at a price nobody would
pay and descends until the first person bids

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:15.990
and thereby wins instantly.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:19.280
And there are two types of sealed bid auctions.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:24.990
In a normal sealed bid auction, everyone submits
their secret bid and the highest one wins

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.520
-- the end.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:31.370
Then there’s the Vickrey Auction, a sealed
bid auction in which the highest bidder actually

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.850
pays the second-highest bid.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.510
But why would you do it that way?!

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.500
Why not just keep it simple?

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:43.850
And how can such a tiny change in the rules
even make a difference?

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:48.340
First, it’s important to note that we’re
dealing with an item with known value.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:54.120
There’s an established retail price for
the PS5, so we’ve got a pretty good sense

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:59.770
of what it’s worth -- millions of people
have paid that price.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:02.440
Compare that to, say… a painting.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.760
I might think it’s a $100 painting.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:11.760
Painting Collector McGee, a person I just
made up, might think it’s a $25,000 painting.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.500
I don’t know.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:14.500
But..

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:19.660
What about if the item is like an oil field
somewhere and no one knows what’s really

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:20.760
under the ground.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:26.470
That makes it tough to establish a value yourself,
but it makes it really hard to know what other

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:28.940
people might be bidding.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:34.010
With a PS5, we’ve got a pretty good idea
of what other people might be thinking.

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:40.120
So let’s say Jake figured he’d be up against
someone who might bid at least as much as

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:47.510
the $550 he was willing to pay… but maybe
they’ll try to get a deal and hope that

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:49.510
everyone else can’t afford it.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:56.090
He thinks they’ve got a bidding range of
$450 to $650, with an equal probability of

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:59.350
submitting any bid within that range.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:04.949
So should he bid $550, so half the time he
wins, half the time he doesn’t, and when

00:06:04.949 --> 00:06:08.100
he loses he’s not really out anything?

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:11.470
In a standard sealed bid auction format…

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:12.470
No.

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:19.240
If he bids over $550 and wins, he’s losing
value by paying more than he wanted to, so

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:20.990
that’s out.

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:28.590
But if he bids under $550 -- say, $530 -- he’ll
still win 40% of the time against the mystery

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:33.280
bidder, and each time he wins he’s saving
$20.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:37.310
If the highest bid was over $550, he would
have lost anyway.

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:43.930
The extra value he gains out of saving $20
4 times out of ten outweighs that one time

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:48.190
he’d bid $530 and lose to a bid of $550.

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:53.220
That’s called ‘bid shading,’ and it’s
what Nobel prize-winner William Vickrey solved

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:58.639
in 1961 in “Counterspeculation, Auctions,
and Competitive Sealed Tenders.”

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:03.860
Vickrey’s work on auctions included the
development of the Revenue Equivalence Theorem,

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:08.690
which showed that if an auction satisfies
a handful of conditions, the revenue for the

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:14.930
seller will be the same no matter what the
format -- and for all this, he found out he

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:21.550
won the 1996 Nobel Prize in Economics just
3 days before he died.

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:25.889
So what’s so great about Vickrey Auctions?

00:07:25.889 --> 00:07:32.160
By making the winner pay the second-highest
bid instead, that incentive to manipulate

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:40.620
the bidding evaporated -- and it actually
started to pay to be as honest as possible.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:46.180
Vickrey ushered in an era of honest bidding
being the dominant strategy in an auction

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:48.530
-- let’s go back to Jake.

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:54.900
He wants to pay $550 for the PS5 because that’s
what it’s worth to him.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:08:00.780
If he commits to the pro gamer move of bidding
$8,000 just to guarantee that he’ll win

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:05.550
the auction, the second highest bidder could
be any number lower than that.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:13.650
He could get stuck paying $600, $800, $4,000,
or more, especially if someone else is trying

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:16.520
to use the same strategy.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:22.100
Inflating his bid not only doesn’t help,
but could put him in a really bad position.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:28.290
If Jake wants to try to save a little money,
he can still bid $550 -- because if he wins,

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:33.779
he’s paying less than that anyway based
on whatever the next-highest bid is.

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:39.500
He doesn’t have to engage in bid shading…
and if he does try to game the system, he’s

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:43.309
susceptible to being outbid and losing.

00:08:43.309 --> 00:08:48.550
In a second-price auction, potential buyers
also have a much harder time colluding with

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:54.610
each other to manipulate the price in their
favor, and sellers just don’t have enough

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:59.600
information to successfully ‘plant’ bids
to drive up a price.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:05.699
In a Vickrey auction, Jake’s incentive to
go higher or lower than his true value is

00:09:05.699 --> 00:09:07.660
just… gone.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:10.029
$550 is his best bid.

00:09:10.029 --> 00:09:15.680
But it’s not just best for him: everyone
he’s bidding against has the same incentive

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:17.980
to bid honestly, too.

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:23.389
The same forces are at work for them, and
they know it, so why not just be honest?

00:09:23.389 --> 00:09:28.819
The seller knows no one’s trying to game
the system, either, and they’re gonna get

00:09:28.819 --> 00:09:31.500
the best price for their item.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.379
And guess what?

00:09:33.379 --> 00:09:36.429
This is pretty much how eBay works.

00:09:36.429 --> 00:09:41.920
Everyone submits their bid and the winner
pays just a tiny bit more than the second-highest

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:42.920
bid.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:48.970
Vickrey had no way to know that almost 60
years after his paper, one single website

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:54.720
on the internet would house nearly a billion
and a half auctions for everyday items worldwide

00:09:54.720 --> 00:10:01.790
-- and virtually everyone would be able to
use his bidding system from a device in their

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:02.790
pockets.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:08.360
The 2020 Nobel prize in Economics went to
Robert B. Wilson and Paul Milgrom for their

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:14.449
work advancing auction theory -- and we’ve
been thinking about a system where I have

00:10:14.449 --> 00:10:19.920
something, you want it, and we need to figure
out how to exchange it in a way that’s best

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:24.459
for both of us for a long time.

00:10:24.459 --> 00:10:31.470
The Greek historian Herodotus wrote about
Babylonian auctions in 2,500 BC, and that's

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:33.980
the most surprising thing here.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:41.630
In 4,500 years of thinking about auctions,
the mathematical development optimizing the

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:48.980
activity of billions of people is really close
to just… being honest.

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:51.170
And in the spirit of honesty...

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:55.449
I’m actually not auctioning my PS5 because
I have Demon’s Souls to play!

00:10:55.449 --> 00:10:57.529
And as always -- thanks for watching.

